  "name": "the quartermaster",
  "description": "Chess in which losing a piece pays you for it, and the pay buys a choice. Every knight, bishop, rook or queen taken puts coins in the purse of the player who lost it, not the one who took it: one for a knight or a bishop, two for a rook, four for a queen; a pawn pays nothing. Instead of moving, a player may spend the whole turn raising a piece onto any empty square of their own back rank: a pawn for one coin, a knight for three, a bishop for four or a rook for six, and each player starts with two coins. The pawn and the knight sell at what they are worth and the long pieces at a coin over, so the question is never just which piece is biggest: a cheap pawn or knight now, while the attack is still coming, or coins saved toward a bishop or a rook that will hold an emptied back rank later. The coins run against the lead, since the side that wins material is the side that is not paid, but the pay is always less than the loss (a rook lost buys back a third of a rook), so trading down is never free, and an attack that strips the defender's pieces has to land before the coins become a wall. The queen is not for sale. A purse holds at most 99.",
